---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const TITLE = "RCE-JEPA: Decoding Intent into an RCE Form";
const ABSTRACT = "RCE-JEPA is intent decoding: it turns stated intent into an RCE Form P = (S, E, δ, D, λ), meaning state, events, transition, derivation and output. RCE handles everything after that: checking, proofs, derivation, emission and placement. The goal is the best performance per clock cycle and per joule. If all code can be encoded in RCE's Form, all code can be produced by JEPA-style or energy-based models over that Form.";
---

<Layout title={TITLE} description={"Research topic. " + ABSTRACT}>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research topic · open</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">{TITLE}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">{ABSTRACT}</p>

    <figure class="mt-10 glass-card p-4">
      <img src="/figures/rce-jepa/pipeline.svg" alt="Pipeline: stated intent, JEPA predictor in representation space, candidate Form P = (S, E, delta, D, lambda), RCE checker, then RCE engine and emit on accept; on reject the checker names the rule and the predictor proposes again" class="w-full rounded bg-white" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-3 font-body text-xs text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">RCE-JEPA pipeline. Intent is embedded and a JEPA predictor estimates the Form's embedding; a decoder proposes energy-ranked candidate Forms P = (S, E, δ, D, λ). The deterministic RCE checker accepts or rejects every candidate, and rejections return structured diagnostics for re-ranking. Only accepted Forms reach the RCE engine for bit-for-bit replay, multi-language emission and placement. The dashed line marks the trust boundary: everything left of it is learned and untrusted. Energy is measured across the whole loop to compute joules per accepted Form. <a href="/figures/rce-jepa/pipeline.png">PNG</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>You say what you want a program to do, and a model turns that into one precise written plan.</li>
        <li>A strict checker reads the plan and either accepts it or says exactly what is wrong. The model never gets the final say.</li>
        <li>Once a plan is accepted, the computer builds the program from it in the way that wastes the least time and electricity.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl text-brand-white">Principles</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      <li>The model only proposes.</li>
      <li>RCE's checker decides.</li>
      <li>Security never depends on the model.</li>
      <li>No model runs inside the RCE toolchain loop.</li>
      <li>Every accepted Form is replayable bit for bit.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed [&_h2]:mt-12 [&_h2]:text-2xl [&_h2]:text-brand-white [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:text-lg [&_h3]:text-brand-white [&_p]:mt-4 [&_ul]:mt-3 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5 [&_li]:mt-2">
    <h2>1. What code generation costs today: measured baselines</h2>
    <p>These are dated measurements on specific models and hardware, quoted from the cited sources. They serve as baselines, not as statements about any current system.</p>
    <h3>Error rates</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>HumanEval (164 hand-written Python problems, unit-tested): Codex-12B solves 28.8% with one sample; with 100 samples it solves 70.2%, and Codex-S reaches 77.5% "by selecting the sample that passes the unit tests". Selecting by mean log-probability without tests gives 44.5% (Chen et al., 2021, abstract and Fig. 1). Pass rate drops "by roughly a factor of 2-3" per additional chained component in a synthetic docstring (Fig. 11).</li>
      <li>SWE-bench (2,294 real GitHub issues across 12 Python repositories): with BM25 retrieval, Claude 2 resolves 1.96% and Claude 3 Opus 3.79%; only 43.07% and 46.56% of their patches apply (Jimenez et al., 2024, Table 5). Of applied but unresolved patches, most are "No-Op" or "Regression" (Table 23).</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Energy</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>LLaMA 65B inference on V100/A100: "about 3-4 Joules for a output token" (Samsi et al., 2023, Sec. IV-C), with node power on the order of 300 W to 1 kW (Sec. IV-B).</li>
      <li>Across 88 models, text generation averages 0.047 kWh per 1,000 inferences; BLOOMz-7B uses 1.0 × 10<sup>-4</sup> kWh (360 J) per inference (Luccioni et al., 2024, Tables 2 and 5). The authors find "multi-purpose, generative architectures are orders of magnitude more expensive than task-specific systems", and that task structure "accounts for more of the variation than the model size does", attributing part of the gap to decision space: a classifier chooses among a few classes, a generator among "an entire vocabulary".</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Opinion.</strong> Taken together these sources suggest the dominant cost of machine-written code is the product of tokens per attempt, joules per token and attempts per accepted program, and that the last factor is large for open-ended generation. The narrower-output-space observation of Luccioni et al. is a convergent observation, not evidence for RCE itself.</p>

    <h2>2. Defined quantities</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Output space.</strong> Free-form generation over vocabulary V with length L has raw space |V|<sup>L</sup>. For RCE, Ω<sub>n</sub> is the set of syntactically valid Forms of size n (Form nodes); Ω<sub>n</sub><sup>✓</sup> ⊆ Ω<sub>n</sub> is the subset that passes the checker. Reported as log<sub>2</sub>|Ω<sub>n</sub>| bits, estimated by grammar counting and sampling.</li>
      <li><strong>Description length.</strong> b(P) is the bits to encode Form P in the canonical Form encoding; b(code) = tokens × log<sub>2</sub>|V| for the equivalent emitted source. Ratio ρ = b(code) / b(P).</li>
      <li><strong>Verification cost.</strong> C<sub>check</sub>(P) is the wall time, cycles and joules for the checker to accept or reject P. Unit tests give C<sub>test</sub>, and a passing test is evidence, not proof.</li>
      <li><strong>Acceptance probability.</strong> a = P(checker accepts and task spec satisfied | intent), measured as pass@1 over the benchmark.</li>
      <li><strong>Joules per accepted program.</strong> J<sub>acc</sub> = (E<sub>model</sub> + E<sub>check</sub> + E<sub>retry</sub>) / N<sub>accepted</sub>, summed over a benchmark run, energy measured at the device, idle subtracted.</li>
      <li><strong>Cycles per accepted program.</strong> K<sub>acc</sub>, the same quantity in CPU/GPU cycles where counters are readable.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Argument (opinion, to be tested).</strong> A Form has five typed slots with fixed composition rules. Constraining output to Ω<sub>n</sub> removes most of |V|<sup>L</sup> before the model is consulted, so fewer bits are predicted per program (ρ &gt; 1), the checker gives a definitive accept or reject in place of a probabilistic test, and rejection is cheap and structured, so retries are guided rather than blind.</p>
    <p><strong>Illustrative arithmetic only, mixing sources.</strong> At 3-4 J/token (Samsi et al.) a 300-token function costs about 0.9-1.2 kJ per sample; at the 28.8% pass@1 of Codex-12B (Chen et al.) that is roughly 3-4 kJ per test-passing function before counting test execution. These numbers come from different models and hardware. RCE-JEPA must beat a properly measured version of this number on the same hardware, not this estimate.</p>

    <h2>3. Why JEPA and energy-based prediction fit</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>I-JEPA predicts "the representations of various target blocks" from a context block; computing the loss in pixel space instead of representation space dropped 1%-ImageNet linear-probe accuracy from 66.9 to 40.7 (Assran et al., 2023, Table 7). A ViT-H/14 was pretrained in under 1,200 GPU hours, reported as over 10× more efficient than MAE.</li>
      <li>V-JEPA 2 casts planning as minimizing an energy, the L1 distance between a predicted future representation and a goal representation (Eq. 5), and reports 16 s per action versus 4 min for a generative video world model on the same GPU (Assran et al., 2025, Table 3), with an energy landscape described as "relatively smooth and locally convex".</li>
      <li>The JEPA framing (LeCun, 2022, as described in I-JEPA Sec. 2): low energy for compatible (x, y) pairs and high energy for incompatible ones, with the loss "applied in embedding space, not input space". The position paper itself could not be retrieved, so it is cited only as described there.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Mapping (opinion).</strong> Intent is x; the Form is y. A context encoder embeds intent, a target encoder embeds Forms, a predictor maps one to the other, and an energy E(intent, P) ranks candidates in Ω<sub>n</sub>. Details irrelevant to meaning (identifier spelling, formatting, emitter language) are supplied deterministically by RCE's emit stage, so the model need not predict them. The checker, not the energy, is the acceptance criterion.</p>

    <h2>4. Falsifiable hypotheses</h2>
    <p>All measured on the benchmark below, same hardware, idle-subtracted joules. Baseline: an autoregressive code LLM of matched parameter count generating source in a target language, judged by that language's tests plus the RCE spec where translatable.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-xs border-collapse [&_td]:border [&_th]:border [&_td]:border-brand-border [&_th]:border-brand-border [&_td]:p-2 [&_th]:p-2 [&_th]:text-left [&_th]:text-brand-white">
      <thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Hypothesis</th><th>Metric</th><th>Supported if</th><th>Disproved if</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>H1</td><td>Form space is much smaller than source space for the same programs</td><td>ρ = b(code)/b(P), median over corpus</td><td>ρ ≥ 5</td><td>ρ &lt; 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>H2</td><td>Checker is cheap relative to generation</td><td>C<sub>check</sub> / E<sub>model</sub> per candidate (joules)</td><td>≤ 0.05</td><td>≥ 0.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>H3</td><td>Representation-space prediction reaches useful acceptance</td><td>pass@1 accepted Forms on held-out intent-to-Form tasks</td><td>≥ baseline pass@1 on the same tasks</td><td>&lt; 0.5 × baseline</td></tr>
        <tr><td>H4</td><td>Energy per accepted program falls</td><td>J<sub>acc</sub>(RCE-JEPA) / J<sub>acc</sub>(baseline)</td><td>≤ 0.1</td><td>≥ 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>H5</td><td>Energy ranking concentrates mass on valid Forms</td><td>top-1 type-check rate; AUROC of E for accept vs reject</td><td>≥ 0.8 top-1 valid; AUROC ≥ 0.85</td><td>AUROC ≤ 0.6</td></tr>
        <tr><td>H6</td><td>The reference apps are encodable</td><td>share of the 6 reference apps plus RCEdb fully expressed as Forms that pass the checker and replay bit-identically</td><td>7 of 7</td><td>any app requires an escape hatch outside the Form</td></tr>
        <tr><td>H7</td><td>Security is model-independent</td><td>adversarial Forms (fuzzed and model-generated) accepted by the checker that violate a stated invariant</td><td>0</td><td>≥ 1 confirmed</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The thesis is disproved overall if H4 fails (no joule advantage per accepted program) or H6 fails (the Form cannot express the reference apps). Either alone is sufficient.</p>

    <h2>5. Risks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Expressiveness ceiling (H6).</strong> If real programs need effects or nondeterminism the Form cannot state, the "all code" premise fails.</li>
      <li><strong>Intent may be the bottleneck.</strong> A smaller output space does not fix underspecified intent. Signal: H3 fails while H1 and H2 hold.</li>
      <li><strong>Spec gaming.</strong> The checker proves a Form is well-formed and meets stated properties, not that it matches what the user meant. Benchmark tasks carry behavioral traces checked by replay; the residual risk is labeled, not solved.</li>
      <li><strong>Corpus scarcity.</strong> Signal: H3 flat as the corpus grows.</li>
      <li><strong>Collapse.</strong> JEPAs need anti-collapse measures (EMA target encoder). A collapsed Form embedding makes H5 fail.</li>
      <li><strong>Measurement error.</strong> Rootless counters (macmon, RAPL) cover package or SoC, not wall power; both arms use the same meter.</li>
      <li><strong>Baseline drift.</strong> The quoted figures come from 2021-2025 systems; a fair test re-measures a current baseline on the same hardware.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What will be delivered</h2>
    <p>Scope, organised as parallel workstreams that meet at the evaluation harness. Every deliverable either produces Forms, produces models that propose Forms, or measures the result.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>W1. Form corpus and dataset card.</strong> Forms paired with intent statements and checker verdicts, from hand-written Forms, reference-app Forms and checker-verified mutations; the card covers provenance, licence, size distribution, negative generation, known gaps and contamination controls; canonical serialization for H1.</li>
      <li><strong>W2. Encoder and predictor.</strong> Intent encoder, EMA Form target encoder, predictor in representation space and energy head E(intent, P), with anti-collapse measures, an embedding-space versus token-space loss ablation, and model cards with measured training energy.</li>
      <li><strong>W3. Checker-constrained decoder.</strong> Grammar-constrained candidate Forms ranked by energy and submitted to the checker, rejection diagnostics fed back under a fixed retry budget; the checker is the sole acceptance authority with no bypass path to the engine.</li>
      <li><strong>W4. Intent-to-Form benchmark.</strong> Unit and composite tasks, the reference apps (game engine, text editor, video player, chat app, browser, terminal) and RCEdb; each task has intent, reference Form, replay traces and an equivalent source-language task for the baseline; held-out split.</li>
      <li><strong>W5. Evaluation harness with energy in joules.</strong> Both arms on identical hardware and tasks; pass@1, pass@k, ρ, C<sub>check</sub>, J<sub>acc</sub>, K<sub>acc</sub>, retries per acceptance, energy-ranking AUROC; rootless counters, idle subtracted, raw logs kept, unavailable counters reported rather than estimated.</li>
      <li><strong>W6. Research article.</strong> This case expanded with results for H1 to H7, including negative results, with figures, tables and reproducibility instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>W7. Open artifacts.</strong> Public: the Form specification slice, checker acceptance rules, benchmark tasks and traces, harness, dataset and model cards, and weights where licences allow. Private: engine source, placement logic and emitter internals. Anything needed to reproduce a reported number is public, or the number is reported as non-reproducible.</li>
      <li><strong>W8. Demo.</strong> Stated intent to predicted Form to checker verdict (including a shown rejection) to engine replay and emitted code in two languages, with an on-screen joule reading.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>Research topic open. The full research case and the initial experiment are in progress. The statement that all code can be produced by models over the Form is a working hypothesis held by OpenIE, not a result.</p>

